annihilator(CoherentSheaf) -- the annihilator ideal
***************************************************

  * Function: *note annihilator: (Saturation)annihilator,
  * Usage: 
        ann F
        annihilator F
  * Inputs:
      * F, a *note coherent sheaf: CoherentSheaf,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Strategy: (Saturation)annihilator, => a *note symbol:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default value null, either Quotient or
        Intersection; see *note annihilator(...,Strategy=>...):
        (Saturation)annihilator,
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, the annihilator ideal

Description
===========

The annihilator of a sheaf of modules (see
https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0H2G ) $\mathcal F$ is the ideal
corresponding to the kernel of the map of sheaves $$ \mathcal O_X \to \mathcal
Hom_{\mathcal O_X}(\mathcal F, \mathcal F).b $$

You may use ann as a synonym for annihilator.

See also
========

  * *note annihilator(Module): (Saturation)annihilator, -- the annihilator
    ideal
  * *note isSupportedInZeroLocus(Ideal,Module):
    (Saturation)isSupportedInZeroLocus, -- whether support of a module is
    contained in the zero locus of the (irrelevant) ideal

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note annihilator(CoherentSheaf): annihilator_lpCoherentSheaf_rp, -- the
    annihilator ideal
