hilbertPolynomial(ProjectiveVariety) -- compute the Hilbert polynomial of the projective variety
************************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note hilbertPolynomial: (Macaulay2Doc)hilbertPolynomial,
  * Usage: 
        hilbertPolynomial V
  * Inputs:
      * V, a *note projective variety: ProjectiveVariety,
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Projective: (Macaulay2Doc)hilbertPolynomial_lp_pd_pd_pd_
        cmProjective=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ..., default value true,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note projective Hilbert polynomial:
        (Macaulay2Doc)ProjectiveHilbertPolynomial,, unless the option
        Projective is false

Description
===========

We compute an example of the *note Hilbert polynomial:
(Macaulay2Doc)hilbertPolynomial, of a projective Hilbert variety. This is the
same as the Hilbert polynomial of its coordinate ring.
+--------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a..d];                          |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = monomialCurveIdeal(R, {1,3,4});    |
|                                            |
|o2 : Ideal of R                             |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i3 : V = Proj(R/I)                          |
|                                            |
|o3 = V                                      |
|                                            |
|o3 : ProjectiveVariety                      |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i4 : h = hilbertPolynomial V                |
|                                            |
|o4 = - 3*P  + 4*P                           |
|          0      1                          |
|                                            |
|o4 : ProjectiveHilbertPolynomial            |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i5 : hilbertPolynomial(V, Projective=>false)|
|                                            |
|o5 = 4i + 1                                 |
|                                            |
|o5 : QQ[i]                                  |
+--------------------------------------------+


These Hilbert polynomials can serve as *note Hilbert functions:
(Macaulay2Doc)hilbertFunction, too since the values of the Hilbert polynomial
eventually are the same as the Hilbert function of the sheaf of rings or of the
underlying ring.
+--------------------------------------------+
|i6 : apply(5, k-> h(k))                     |
|                                            |
|o6 = {1, 5, 9, 13, 17}                      |
|                                            |
|o6 : List                                   |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i7 : apply(5, k-> hilbertFunction(k,ring V))|
|                                            |
|o7 = {1, 4, 9, 13, 17}                      |
|                                            |
|o7 : List                                   |
+--------------------------------------------+

